1 FORTRAN

   Compiles VAX FORTRAN source files into VAX MACRO-32 source files
   (.MAR).  MACRO assembles each into an object module, and LINK links
   the objects into an image RUN runs.

   Format

     FORTRAN filespec[{,|+}...]

2 Parameter

 filespec[{,|+}...]

   The source file, or several separated by commas, each compiled to its
   own MACRO file.  Files joined by + are one compilation, to one MACRO
   file named for the first, and a file followed by /LIBRARY is a text
   library (.TLB by default) for that compilation's INCLUDE statements.
   Without a file type a source is .FOR, or .F90 if there is no .FOR
   file.  A .F90 file is in free source form (Fortran 90's), any other
   in fixed form (VAX FORTRAN's), unless /SOURCE_FORM says otherwise.
   Free form is read (statements anywhere on a line, ! comments, ;
   between statements, & to continue); see Fortran_90 for what of
   Fortran 90 is compiled so far.  Files are found by MACRO's
   rules (see MACRO Parameter); quote a host path to keep its case.

2 Description

   A program compiles, links, and runs in four commands:

     $ FORTRAN SQUARES         ! SQUARES.FOR -> SQUARES.MAR
     $ MACRO SQUARES           ! SQUARES.MAR -> SQUARES.OBJ
     $ LINK SQUARES            ! SQUARES.OBJ -> SQUARES.EXE
     $ RUN SQUARES

   (FORTRAN/OBJECT runs MACRO too.)  The MACRO file is meant to be read:
   it holds the FORTRAN source as comments, each line with its number,
   so the code and the FORTRAN it came from are side by side.  A comment
   names each INCLUDE file, whose lines appear only with INCLUDE's
   /LIST, with their include depth before their numbers.

   The compiler reports each problem it finds with the line it is on:

     %FORT-E-NOTIMPL, the DICTIONARY statement is not implemented yet
       at line 7 of DUA1:[WORK]PROG.FOR;1
             DICTIONARY 'PARTS'
       ------^

   After an error (E) or a fatal error (F) no MACRO file is written, so
   a failed compilation never replaces a good one, and the command ends
   with DCL-E-FORERRORS.  After warnings (W) the file is written and
   $STATUS is a warning.  With several sources, each failure is shown as
   it happens and the others are still compiled.  The compiler checks
   the program units of one file against each other: a function's type
   where it is used, and the number of arguments of each call.

   From the host shell, "govax fortran filespec..." runs this command,
   with --macro file or --no-macro, --extend-source, --d-lines,
   --continuations n, --no-i4, --g-floating, --align value, --no-align,
   --optimize, --no-optimize, --list, --list-file file, --show keywords,
   --cross-reference, --check keywords, --warnings keywords, --standard
   keywords, --debug keywords, --object, --object-file file, --no-f77,
   --recursive, --f90, --no-f90, and --source-form form.

2 Language

   The compiler takes VAX FORTRAN V5.0, FORTRAN-77 with DEC's
   extensions: main programs, subroutines, functions of every type,
   ENTRY, and BLOCK DATA; declarations of every type, IMPLICIT,
   PARAMETER, arrays, COMMON, EQUIVALENCE, DATA, SAVE, EXTERNAL, and
   INTRINSIC; DEC's STRUCTURE, RECORD, UNION, and MAP, octal,
   hexadecimal, Hollerith, and RADIX-50 constants, VOLATILE, AUTOMATIC,
   and STATIC, and the CDEC$ directives (IDENT, PSECT, TITLE, SUBTITLE,
   OPTIONS, and END OPTIONS); assignment and expressions in every type
   and length (BYTE, LOGICAL, INTEGER, REAL in F, D or G, and H, COMPLEX,
   and CHARACTER, with substrings and concatenation), and every
   intrinsic function, in line or by MTHRTL; every IF, DO, and GO TO,
   CALL, RETURN, STOP, and PAUSE; READ, WRITE, and the other I/O
   statements, and OPEN and the other file statements, on sequential,
   relative, and indexed files, the keyed READ with every key specifier
   among them.  DICTIONARY draws %FORT-E-NOTIMPL.

   From a later DEC FORTRAN, a FUNCTION or SUBROUTINE may be RECURSIVE
   (RECURSIVE INTEGER FUNCTION FACT(N) RESULT(F)), as may every one in a
   file under /RECURSIVE or after OPTIONS /RECURSIVE.  Such a routine may
   call itself, and each activation has its own variables, but for those
   SAVE'd, given initial values, in COMMON, or STATIC, and its own
   temporaries, so an AST or a handler may also enter it while it runs.
   In the routine, its name with an argument list is a call.  Without
   RESULT the name alone is the function's value; RESULT(name) names
   another variable for it instead.  AUTOMATIC alone doesn't make a
   routine safe to enter twice: its temporaries stay static.

   A program can use VMS directly: a system service or RTL routine is a
   routine like any other, called as a function for its status, with
   arguments left out (STATUS = SYS$GETJPIW(,,,LIST,IOSB,,)), %VAL,
   %REF, %DESCR, and %LOC.  A FORTRAN function can be a condition
   handler (LIB$ESTABLISH), and a subroutine an AST routine or exit
   handler.  The system subroutines are there: DATE, TIME, IDATE, EXIT,
   SECNDS, RAN, MVBITS, and ERRSNS.

2 Fortran_90

   Fortran 90 (ISO/IEC 1539:1991) is being added to the same compiler,
   a part at a time.  So far a program may be written in free form, by
   a .F90 file's type or /SOURCE_FORM=FREE, and with Fortran 90's
   spellings:

     program squares                ! ! starts a comment anywhere
       implicit none
       integer i
       do i = 1, 3; write (*, 10) i, i*i; end do   ! ; between statements
     10 format (1x, "square of", i2, " is", i3)
       if (i == 4 .and. i /= 5) print *, "done, &
                                         &it's ", i  ! & continues
     end program squares

   That is: the relational operators ==, /=, <, <=, >, and >= (written
   .EQ. and the rest in fixed form too); character constants between
   quotation marks under /F90, in expressions and FORMAT statements;
   B'101', O'17', and Z'1F'; END PROGRAM, END SUBROUTINE, and END
   FUNCTION with or without the unit's name, which must be its own.  A
   blank may not stand inside a name or a keyword in free form, and must
   stand between two names (GOTO and GO TO are both allowed, as are the
   standard's other compounds).  A statement may have 39 continuation
   lines, and a line 132 characters.  Both forms may make up one
   program, and an INCLUDE file is read in the form of its own type.

   Type declarations may have Fortran 90's :: form, with attributes and
   values given with =:  INTEGER, PARAMETER :: N = 10 and REAL,
   DIMENSION(N), SAVE :: X = 0.  PARAMETER, DIMENSION, SAVE, EXTERNAL,
   INTRINSIC, VOLATILE, AUTOMATIC, INTENT, OPTIONAL, and STATIC are
   compiled; TARGET is checked and recorded; each has its own
   statement, with or without ::.  A value given with = fills a whole
   array and gives the name SAVE.

   A type may have a kind in parentheses, a number of bytes:
   INTEGER(2) is INTEGER*2, REAL(KIND=8) is REAL*8, COMPLEX(8) (the
   parts' kind) is COMPLEX*16, and CHARACTER(LEN=10, KIND=1) is
   CHARACTER*10.  A constant may have one too, digits or a constant's
   name: 7_2, 1.5_DP (read at REAL*8's precision), .TRUE._1, 1_'TEXT'.
   INTEGER(1), also written INTEGER*1, is a signed byte, its
   operations done in bytes; BYTE is still LOGICAL*1.  COMPLEX(16),
   also written COMPLEX*32, has two REAL(16) parts: its arithmetic,
   comparisons, and conversions are compiled, and INT, REAL, DBLE,
   QEXT, CMPLX, DCMPLX, AIMAG, and CONJG take one, but its I/O, its
   powers, and the other intrinsic functions of one are not there yet.
   (1.0_16, 0.0) is a COMPLEX(16) constant.  IMPLICIT takes kinds too:
   IMPLICIT REAL(8) (A-H, O-Z).

   The kind functions KIND, SELECTED_INT_KIND, and SELECTED_REAL_KIND,
   and the numeric inquiries HUGE, TINY, EPSILON, DIGITS, PRECISION,
   RANGE, RADIX, MINEXPONENT, MAXEXPONENT, and BIT_SIZE, are answered
   when compiling, from the VAX formats' models, so they may name kinds
   and PARAMETERs.  Without /G_FLOATING, SELECTED_REAL_KIND(15, 307) is
   16 (H), since D's range is F's.  INT, NINT, REAL, CMPLX, AINT, ANINT,
   and LOGICAL take a KIND for their result, and these functions take
   keyword arguments: SELECTED_REAL_KIND(R=40), INT(X, KIND=2).  Under
   /NOF90 the new names are a program's own unless named INTRINSIC.

   A DO loop, block IF, or SELECT CASE construct may be named (OUTER:
   DO ... END DO OUTER, with the name allowed on ELSE IF, ELSE, and
   CASE too); a named construct must be named at its end, and its name
   may name nothing else in the unit.  DO alone has no loop control and
   runs until something leaves it.  EXIT leaves a loop and CYCLE goes on
   to its next pass, the innermost loop around them or the one they name
   (EXIT OUTER leaves every loop inside OUTER too); IF (X < 0) EXIT is a
   single branch.

     select case (ch)
     case ('a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u')
        vowels = vowels + 1
     case ('b':'d', 'f':'h', 'j':'n', 'p':'t', 'v':'z')
        consonants = consonants + 1
     case default
        others = others + 1
     end select

   SELECT CASE chooses a block by an INTEGER, CHARACTER, or LOGICAL
   value, computed once: CASE (1, 3:5, 10:), CASE (:-1), CASE ('A':'Z'),
   CASE (.TRUE.), and CASE DEFAULT for every value no other case takes;
   with no match and no DEFAULT nothing runs.  Ranges may not overlap
   (CASEOVERLAP), and one that can never match draws the warning
   CASENEVER.  A BYTE selector is LOGICAL*1, so declare it INTEGER(1) to
   select it by number.  An INTEGER construct becomes a CASEL table, a
   binary search, or a chain of comparisons in the order written,
   whichever suits its values.  A GO TO may jump to END IF from any
   block of its IF or from outside, under /NOF90 too.

   Arrays are values: a whole array or a section may stand in an
   expression or assignment wherever a scalar may, each element done in
   turn, and a scalar goes with any array.

     curved = min(score * 1.1, 100.0)       ! every element
     score(2:n) = score(1:n-1)              ! shifts: as if computed first
     where (curved < 60.0) curved = 60.0    ! only where the mask holds
     total = sum(a(:, j), mask=a(:, j) > 0)

   A section's subscripts may be triplets, low:high:stride with any part
   left out (A(:, J), A(N:1:-1)), or vector subscripts, INTEGER arrays
   of subscripts (A(IV)).  Two arrays in one operation must have one
   shape (NOTCONFORM).  (/ 1, 2, (I, I = 3, N) /) is an array
   constructor; a PARAMETER may be an array, of rank 2 by RESHAPE.  The
   elemental functions (SQRT, MAX, MERGE, ...) take arrays; SUM,
   PRODUCT, MAXVAL, MINVAL, ALL, ANY, and COUNT take MASK= and DIM=;
   MAXLOC, MINLOC, DOT_PRODUCT, MATMUL, TRANSPOSE, SIZE, SHAPE, LBOUND,
   and UBOUND are there too, and SPREAD, CSHIFT, EOSHIFT, PACK, UNPACK,
   and RESHAPE of any values.  WHERE is a statement or a construct with
   ELSEWHERE.  Sections and array expressions in I/O lists are read or
   written an element at a time.  A statement that needs a temporary
   array gets one of the size it needs, on the stack up to 64 KB and
   from the heap past that, given back at the statement's end.  A
   section may be passed to any procedure (below).

   A main program or subprogram may end with CONTAINS and internal
   procedures (END SUBROUTINE, END FUNCTION), which use their host's
   names: a name the host uses anywhere, even only in a DO, is the
   host's unless the internal procedure declares its own.  An interface
   block (INTERFACE ... END INTERFACE) describes external and dummy
   procedures by their FUNCTION or SUBROUTINE statements and
   declarations.  Through either explicit interface every reference is
   checked (ARGCOUNT, ARGTYPE, ARGRANK, ARGLENGTH, NOTDEFINABLE), may
   give arguments by keyword, and may leave out OPTIONAL dummies, which
   PRESENT asks about; a left-out argument is passed as 0, and a shorter
   argument list from a MACRO or old FORTRAN caller is understood.

     call solve(x, n, tol=1.0e-6)
     if (present(tol)) eps = tol

   INTENT(IN) dummies can't be given values (INTENTIN), and an
   INTENT(OUT) one used before anything sets it draws INTENTOUTREF.
   INTERFACE name makes a generic name, each reference choosing the
   specific its arguments fit exactly, by type, kind, and rank;
   INTERFACE OPERATOR(.op.) and OPERATOR(+) give operators meanings for
   operands Fortran gives none (or only VAX FORTRAN does), and
   INTERFACE ASSIGNMENT(=) defines assignments Fortran doesn't.

   An ALLOCATABLE array, declared with a colon for each dimension, gets
   its bounds and memory from ALLOCATE, gives them back by DEALLOCATE,
   and ALLOCATED says which it is now:

     real, allocatable :: g(:, :)
     allocate (g(n, 0:m))
     if (allocated(g)) deallocate (g)

   Allocating one already allocated is %FOR-F-ALREADYALLOC, deallocating
   one that isn't %FOR-F-NOTALLOC, and too large %FOR-F-INSVIRMEM; with
   STAT=ist the statement stores 0 or the FOR number (301, 302, 41) of
   its first failure instead.  A subprogram's allocatable arrays not
   SAVE'd are deallocated at each of its returns.

   A subprogram's own array may have bounds computed when it is entered,
   from its dummy arguments, common, and SIZE, LBOUND, UBOUND, or LEN of
   them: an automatic array, made on the stack and gone at the return.

     real :: w(0:size(a) + 1)

   It can't be in COMMON, EQUIVALENCE, DATA, NAMELIST, SAVE, or STATIC
   (%FORT-E-AUTOATTR).

   A CHARACTER length may be computed the same way, when the subprogram is
   entered: a dummy argument's is the first N characters of its actual
   argument, and the subprogram's own names with one are made on the stack.

     character(len=n) :: s
     character(len=len(c) + 1) :: t, w(n)

   The same rules hold (%FORT-E-AUTOLENATTR), and a main program's lengths
   must be constant (%FORT-E-LENNOTDUMMY).

   A dummy array declared with a colon for each dimension takes its
   shape from its actual argument, a whole array or a section with any
   steps; its lower bounds are its own, 1 or as declared:

     real :: a(:, :), b(0:)
     call halve(x(1:9:2, :))

   It goes by a class NCA descriptor, so a reference needs an explicit
   interface (an internal procedure's, an interface block's;
   %FORT-E-EXPLICITREQ otherwise), and the actual must be an array of its
   rank (%FORT-E-ARGRANK).  An array expression, a section with a vector
   subscript, or (A) is computed into a temporary, which is passed and
   not copied back.  Under /CHECK=BOUNDS the routine refuses anything but
   a class NCA or class A descriptor of the dummy's rank
   (%FOR-F-BADARRDESC).

   A section or array expression passed to a dummy that isn't
   assumed-shape (or to a routine with no interface) goes as the
   address of elements one after another: a section that already is
   one, A(:, J), where it is; any other through a temporary, copied back
   after the call unless the dummy is INTENT(IN) or the actual an
   expression or vector-subscripted.  An assumed-shape array is tested
   when the program runs and copied only if it must be; it may also be a
   run-time format, an internal file, or ENCODE's or DECODE's buffer.

   A function's value may be an array, its name (or RESULT variable)
   given dimensions:

     function cross(a, b)
       real :: a(3), b(3), cross(3)

   A reference needs an explicit interface (%FORT-E-EXPLICITVALUE
   otherwise); its value is an array expression of that shape, computed
   once, before its statement's loops, into a temporary the call passes
   as a hidden first argument.  A value's bounds may be specification
   expressions (REAL F(N)); then the function makes the value on the
   heap and describes it in a class NCA descriptor the caller passes,
   and the caller gives it back at the statement's end.  A CHARACTER
   value's length may be a specification expression too; a scalar one
   is a dynamic string in a class D descriptor the caller passes.  ENTRY
   may name such a function's entry points, each declared alike
   (%FORT-E-ENTRYVALUE, %FORT-E-ENTRYMAKE).

   Under /DEBUG the debugger's EXAMINE reads allocatable, automatic, and
   assumed-shape arrays through their descriptors, a section by its
   strides (%DEBUG-W-NOTALLOC for an array not allocated).

   Fortran 90's other statements (MODULE, POINTER, and the rest) are
   recognized and draw %FORT-E-NOTIMPL until they are
   added.  A format written as a character constant may
   hold "..." constants under /F90; one in a variable can't yet, and
   FTNRTL refuses it, %FOR-F-SYNERRFOR.

2 Programs

   A program may be several files, each compiled and assembled on its
   own, then linked together.  A common block of one name is the same
   storage in each unit that declares it, as long as the longest
   declaration.  A BLOCK DATA may give it initial values from another
   file; name the BLOCK DATA in an EXTERNAL statement, so that LINK
   reports it if it's missing and finds it in a library:

     $ FORTRAN STORE,SHOW,START
     $ MACRO STORE,SHOW,START
     $ LINK STORE,SHOW,START

   INCLUDE 'file' reads a file, by default of type .FOR, found relative
   to the file that includes it: on the host, in its directory; on a
   volume, on its device and in its directory.  INCLUDE 'file/LIST' puts
   the file's lines in the MACRO file; /NOLIST is the default.

   INCLUDE '(module)' reads a text library's module (see LIBRARY), from
   the first of these that has it: the libraries given with the source
   (FORTRAN PROG+MYLIB/LIBRARY), the library the logical name
   FORT$LIBRARY names, SYS$LIBRARY:FORSYSDEF.TLB, and last the definition
   modules built into govax, such as $SSDEF, $IODEF, $JPIDEF, $FORDEF,
   $CHFDEF, and $SYSSRVNAM, whose INTEGER*4 and PARAMETER statements give
   VMS's names their values.  INCLUDE 'lib(module)' reads only that
   library.

2 FTNRTL

   Compiled programs call FTNRTL, govax's FORTRAN run-time library, a
   shareable image written in MACRO-32 and built into govax.  LINK finds
   its routines (named FTN$...) without an options file, and RUN finds
   the image.  Both look for a file of one's own first: a logical name
   FTNRTL, SYS$SHARE:FTNRTL.EXE, or FTNRTL.EXE in the host library
   directory.  The system volume has its sources, and
   SYS$SOURCES:BUILDFTN.COM, which rebuilds it.

   FTNRTL has FTN$VERSION, FTN$STOP, the I/O statements' calls
   (FTN$READ_SF, FTN$WRITE_SF, and the other begin calls, FTN$IO_ITEM,
   FTN$IO_END), ISHFTC (FTN$IISHFTC, FTN$JISHFTC), and a routine for each
   intrinsic function MTHRTL lacks, for a program that passes the
   function's name as an argument (FTN$LEN, FTN$INDEX, ...,
   FTN$NWORKERS), which MACRO programs can call too.

   List-directed output goes to SYS$OUTPUT in records of 80 bytes, each
   starting with a blank (its carriage control), each value in its
   type's format: LOGICAL as T or F; an integer in 5 columns for BYTE
   and LOGICAL*1, 7 for INTEGER*2, and 12 for INTEGER*4; REAL with 7
   significant digits, REAL*8 with 16 (15 with /G_FLOATING), REAL*16
   with 33; COMPLEX as its two parts in parentheses, as
   (1.000000,-2.500000); and CHARACTER as its text.

2 Qualifiers
/ALIGN

      /[NO]ALIGN[=value]

   How structures and common blocks are laid out.  /NOALIGN, the
   default, packs them, as VAX FORTRAN V5.0 does.  /ALIGN puts each
   field and common name on its natural boundary.  A value is ALL, NONE,
   NATURAL, PACKED, or class=rule, and several go in parentheses: the
   classes are RECORDS (or STRUCTURES) and COMMONS, and the rules
   PACKED, NATURAL, and for COMMONS STANDARD, at most a longword.  /ALIGN
   alone is ALL.  CDEC$ OPTIONS groups in the source override it.

/CHECK

      /[NO]CHECK[=(keyword[,...])]

   What the compiled code looks out for: ALL, NONE, [NO]BOUNDS
   (subscripts and substrings: the subscript range trap; an adjustable
   array's bounds: FOR$_ADJARRDIM; a section's first and last
   subscripts, and Fortran 90 array shapes known only at run time:
   FOR$_NOTCONFOR), [NO]OVERFLOW (integer overflow
   traps), and [NO]UNDERFLOW (floating underflow faults).  /CHECK alone
   is ALL.  The default is /NOCHECK.  An OPTIONS statement before a unit
   may change it for that unit.

/CONTINUATIONS

      /CONTINUATIONS=n

   The most continuation lines one statement may have, from 0 to 99.
   The default is 19 in fixed form and 39 in free form.

/CROSS_REFERENCE

      /[NO]CROSS_REFERENCE

   A listing follows each unit's map with where its names and labels
   are mentioned: a line number each, with D if the line defines the
   name and A if it assigns it; 1:3 is line 3 of a file included at
   depth 1.  It is there after errors too.  Without /LIST it does
   nothing but say so.  The default is /NOCROSS_REFERENCE.

/D_LINES

      /[NO]D_LINES

   Compiles the debugging lines (those with a D in column 1) as
   statements.  The default is /NOD_LINES: they are comments.

/DEBUG

      /[NO]DEBUG[=(keyword[,...])]

   What MACRO is to write for the debugger: ALL, NONE, [NO]SYMBOLS
   (debugger records), and [NO]TRACEBACK (a traceback's names).  /DEBUG
   alone is ALL, so a plain MACRO then writes debugger records.
   /NODEBUG, the default, is traceback alone.  It doesn't turn the
   optimizer off: give /NOOPTIMIZE as well to debug.

/EXTEND_SOURCE

      /[NO]EXTEND_SOURCE

   Lets the statement field of a fixed-format line run to column 132
   instead of column 72.  The default is /NOEXTEND_SOURCE.  An OPTIONS
   statement overrides this for the unit after it.

/F77

      /[NO]F77

   /NOF77 is FORTRAN-66's reading of what FORTRAN-77 changed: a DO runs
   at least once, OPEN's defaults are STATUS='NEW' and BLANK='ZERO', and
   EXTERNAL means a library function of that name unless written *name.
   The default is /F77.  An OPTIONS statement before a unit may change it
   for that unit.

/F90

      /[NO]F90

   Where Fortran 90 and VAX FORTRAN read the same text differently, /F90
   takes Fortran 90's reading: "..." is a character constant (not an
   octal number), TYPE name begins a derived type, and Fortran 90's new
   intrinsic names (SUM, SIZE, TRIM, ...) are intrinsic.  Fortran 90's
   other syntax needs no qualifier.  The default is /F90 for a free-form
   source and /NOF90 for a fixed-form one, so an old .FOR file compiles
   as it always has.  An OPTIONS statement before a unit may change it
   for that unit, written in full (/F is still /F77).  So far "..."
   constants are read, in FORMAT statements too; derived types and the
   new intrinsics are being added (see Fortran_90).

/G_FLOATING

      /[NO]G_FLOATING

   REAL*8 (DOUBLE PRECISION) values are G_floating: 53 bits of
   precision, about 15 decimal digits, and a range of about 0.56E-308 to
   0.9E308.  With /NOG_FLOATING, the default, they are D_floating: 56
   bits, about 16 digits, and REAL's range, about 0.29E-38 to 1.7E38.  A
   unit's OPTIONS statement overrides this for that unit; the units of a
   program must agree, since a value passed from one format to the other
   is misread.

/HOST

   After a source, makes it a host path, whatever it looks like.

/I4

      /[NO]I4

   INTEGER and LOGICAL with no length, and implied integers, are 4 bytes
   (the default).  With /NOI4 they are 2, and an integer constant from
   -32768 to 32767 is an INTEGER*2.  A unit's OPTIONS statement
   overrides this for that unit.

/LIBRARY

   After a source joined to others by +, makes it a text library for
   that compilation's INCLUDE '(module)' statements.

/LIST

      /[NO]LIST[=filespec]

   Writes a listing: the source's lines with their numbers, each message
   under its line, and each unit's storage map.  By default it is the
   source's name with the type LIS, in the default directory (see
   SPECIFY Output_files), and it is written after errors too.  The default
   is /NOLIST.  CDEC$ TITLE and SUBTITLE set the heading of the page after
   the one they are on; with no TITLE, a page's title is its unit's name.

/MACRO

      /[NO]MACRO[=filespec]

   Writes the MACRO file (the default).  Without a file name, it has the
   source's name, with the type MAR in the same case as the source's
   type (squares.for gives squares.mar), in the default directory, not
   beside the source (see SPECIFY Output_files).  A name is placed
   as MACRO's /OBJECT places its object, and a VMS name without a type
   gets MAR.  On a volume, the file is the next version of its name.  A
   host source whose type is already MAR, in the current directory, is
   refused unless /MACRO names another file, since its MACRO file would
   replace it.  /NOMACRO
   compiles the source and reports any errors, but writes no MACRO file.

/OBJECT

      /[NO]OBJECT[=filespec]

   /OBJECT runs MACRO on the MACRO file too, for an object file, by
   default the source's name with type OBJ.  The MACRO file is kept, so
   /OBJECT can't go with /NOMACRO.  The default is /NOOBJECT.

/OPTIMIZE

      /[NO]OPTIMIZE

   Runs the optimizer's passes, for code that does what the program
   says in fewer instructions.  The passes run are named in the MACRO
   file's header.  The default is /OPTIMIZE.

/RECURSIVE

      /[NO]RECURSIVE

   Every FUNCTION and SUBROUTINE may be active more than once at a time,
   as if each said RECURSIVE: its local variables are in its stack frame
   unless SAVE'd, given initial values, in COMMON, or STATIC.  The
   default is /NORECURSIVE.  An OPTIONS statement before a unit may
   change it for that unit.

/SHOW

      /[NO]SHOW[=(keyword[,...])]

   What a listing holds: ALL, NONE, [NO]INCLUDE (every INCLUDE file's
   lines, whatever INCLUDE's own /LIST says), and [NO]MAP.  /SHOW alone
   is ALL and /NOSHOW NONE; with no /SHOW, a listing has the maps, and
   only the INCLUDE files that say /LIST.

/SOURCE_FORM

      /SOURCE_FORM=keyword

   The form of the source files named, whatever their types say: FREE
   (Fortran 90's free form) or FIXED (VAX FORTRAN's columns and tab
   format).  Without it a .F90 file is free form and any other fixed.
   A file named in an INCLUDE statement always has its own type's form.

/STANDARD

      /[NO]STANDARD[=(keyword[,...])]

   Flags what FORTRAN-77 doesn't have, each an informational message, so
   the program still compiles: SYNTAX (statements, constants, operators,
   specifiers, edit descriptors, names, intrinsic functions),
   SOURCE_FORM (tabs, small letters, ! comments, debugging lines, past
   column 72, over 19 continuation lines), ALL, NONE, and NOSYNTAX and
   NOSOURCE_FORM.  /STANDARD alone is ALL.  The default is /NOSTANDARD.

/WARNINGS

      /[NO]WARNINGS[=(keyword[,...])]

   What the compiler says, beyond errors: ALL, NONE, [NO]GENERAL (its
   warnings and informational messages; on by default),
   [NO]DECLARATIONS (each name with no type of its own), [NO]UNUSED
   (names only declared, and labels nothing refers to),
   [NO]ALIGNMENT (fields and common names off their natural
   boundaries), and [NO]ARGUMENT_CHECKING (an argument whose type,
   kind, or rank isn't its dummy's, in a reference without an
   explicit interface to a routine of the same file).  /WARNINGS alone is ALL and /NOWARNINGS is NONE.  What
   isn't shown doesn't count: a compilation with only those ends with a
   good status.

2 Examples

   1.$ FORTRAN SQUARES

     Compiles SQUARES.FOR into SQUARES.MAR.

   2.$ FORTRAN DUA1:[SRC]PROG /MACRO=DUA1:[OBJ]

     Writes PROG.MAR in another directory.

   3.$ FORTRAN A,B,C

     Compiles three files, each to its own MACRO file.

   4.$ FORTRAN PROG+DEFS/LIBRARY

     Compiles PROG.FOR, its INCLUDE '(module)' statements reading
     DEFS.TLB first.

   5.$ FORTRAN PROG /ALIGN=(RECORDS=NATURAL,COMMONS=STANDARD)

     Lays out structures naturally, and common blocks at most on
     longwords.

   6.$ FORTRAN PROG /LIST /CROSS_REFERENCE /SHOW=INCLUDE

     Writes PROG.LIS with a cross reference and every INCLUDE file's
     lines.

   7.$ FORTRAN PROG /CHECK=BOUNDS /WARNINGS=(DECLARATIONS,UNUSED)

     Checks subscripts as the program runs, and reports names without
     types and names never used.

   8.$ FORTRAN/DEBUG/NOOPTIMIZE/OBJECT PROG
     $ LINK/DEBUG PROG
     $ RUN PROG

     Builds PROG for the debugger, and runs it under the debugger.

   9.% govax --mount-write DUA1=work.dsk fortran DUA1:[000000]PROG.FOR

     Compiles a volume's file from the host shell.
